<section class="three-tool-knowledge" id="dhul-deep-guide"><h2>Dhul Hijjah Planner: first ten days, Arafah, Eid and Tashreeq</h2><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Dhul Hijjah is the twelfth Hijri month. The first ten days are especially significant for worship, 9 Dhul Hijjah is the day of Arafah and 10 Dhul Hijjah is Eid al-Adha. The planner also marks 11–13 as the days of Tashreeq. All dates are calculated estimates until relevant official lunar calendar announcements.</p><nav class="three-tool-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#dhul-deep-merit">Why are the first ten days</a><a href="#dhul-deep-day">Arafah, Eid and Tashreeq d</a><a href="#dhul-deep-fasting">Which Dhul Hijjah days may</a><a href="#dhul-deep-qurbani">When does Qurbani take pla</a><a href="#dhul-deep-steps">How to make a Dhul Hijjah </a><a href="#dhul-deep-sources">Sources</a></nav><div class="three-tool-facts"><div><strong>First ten days: 1–10</strong></div><div><strong>Arafah: 9</strong></div><div><strong>Tashreeq: 11–13</strong></div></div><section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-merit"><h3>Why are the first ten days of Dhul Hijjah special?</h3><p>Sahih al-Bukhari 969 describes the special merit of good deeds during the first ten days of Dhul Hijjah. Practical preparations may include prayer, charity, Quran reading, remembrance and fasting on eligible days. These are not fixed daily quotas imposed by the app.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-day"><h3>Arafah, Eid and Tashreeq dates explained</h3><p>Arafah is conventionally 9 Dhul Hijjah and central to Hajj. Eid al-Adha begins on 10 Dhul Hijjah. The 11th, 12th and 13th are the days of Tashreeq. The planner shows the full first thirteen days so users do not mistake every Dhul Hijjah date for an optional fast.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-fasting"><h3>Which Dhul Hijjah days may be fasted?</h3><p>Voluntary fasting during appropriate early Dhul Hijjah days is encouraged in many scholarly discussions, with particular attention to Arafah for those not performing Hajj, subject to relevant views. Do not treat Eid al-Adha or ordinary Tashreeq days as ordinary voluntary fasts. Pilgrims on Arafah have different considerations.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-qurbani"><h3>When does Qurbani take place?</h3><p>Qurbani or Udhiyah is tied to Eid al-Adha and the permitted slaughter days; juristic views differ on the final valid day and other conditions. An estimated Islamic calendar does not certify a provider's timings or a sacrifice's validity. Use the separate Qurbani Shares tool to plan finances only.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-steps"><h3>How to make a Dhul Hijjah calendar</h3><p>Choose a Hijri year, review the 1–13 Dhul Hijjah rows and highlight Arafah, Eid and Tashreeq. Optionally inspect a labelled earlier/later day illustration. Export the canonical calculation as CSV or an ICS all-day planning reminder, or print the table. Recheck authoritative lunar-date announcements before sharing times or making commitments.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: the important 9–13 Dhul Hijjah sequence</h3><p>Take any selected Hijri year. Day 9 is labelled Arafah, day 10 Eid al-Adha, and days 11–13 Tashreeq. The five entries use consecutive civil-date labels in the chosen calendar. The app never treats the three Tashreeq dates as ordinary White Days fasts, even where day 13 overlaps the lunar monthly 13th.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-hajj"><h3>Dhul Hijjah planning for a Hajj pilgrim</h3><p>Pilgrims' movements between Mina, Arafah and Muzdalifah follow detailed instructions and schedules. The day-number table is not a complete route, safety briefing or ritual checklist. Follow your authorised Hajj group, Saudi guidance and a qualified religious guide for pilgrimage timing and practices.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-uncertainty"><h3>Why Saudi and local Dhul Hijjah dates may differ</h3><p>The calendar model is Umm al-Qura, while the actual month and Eid declarations depend on the authority followed. A one-day illustrative shift cannot authenticate an announced date. The ICS file contains calculated all-day entries, not a sunset alarm or a guarantee of when Eid or Arafah will be observed.</p></section><section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-faq"><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details class="faq-detail"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}</section><section class="three-tool-section" id="dhul-deep-sources"><h3>Sources and further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:969" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 969</a></li><li><a href="https://quran.com/89/1-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 89:1-2</a></li><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1141a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1141a</a></li><li><a href="https://quran.com/22/36" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 22:36</a></li></ul><p class="small">Educational reference, not a binding religious ruling. Qualified scholarly review pending.</p></section></section>